      {
        "id": "ordinary-life",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding culture through ordinary life",
        "content": "A café meeting, a shopping trip, and time spent in a public park reveal different aspects of everyday culture. They show how people use space, spend resources, and sustain relationships. Photographs made by Associated Press in Tehran in April 2026 document examples of these activities, although a photographic visit cannot establish how common they are across Iran. [s1]\n\nContemporary culture is therefore best approached through particular settings and experiences. Research in Zanjan, a provincial capital in northwestern Iran, found that social class influenced participation in urban leisure and consumption spaces. Such findings challenge descriptions of an undifferentiated Iranian lifestyle, while remaining evidence about a specific city and research period. [s2]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1",
          "s2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "urban-and-consumer-life",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Urban life and consumer life overlap",
        "content": "The Urban Culture branch examines how shared places support everyday interaction. Consumer Culture asks how shopping, leisure, and the meanings attached to goods and places shape participation. These subjects overlap: a commercial space can also be a meeting place, while access to leisure can depend on what someone can afford. The Zanjan study provides a local example of that connection. [s2]\n\nHistorical commercial institutions also remain relevant to this distinction. UNESCO describes the Tabriz Historic Bazaar Complex as an interconnected environment with commercial, social, educational, and religious functions. Its significance lies partly in the relationship between buildings and professional organization, rather than architecture alone. This example helps explain why separating economic activity from cultural life can obscure how a place works. [s3]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s2",
          "s3"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "digital-participation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Digital participation and its constraints",
        "content": "Internet use supplies a measurable dimension of changing everyday life. The inspected International Telecommunication Union series, distributed through UNdata, records rising national participation over the displayed historical period. It measures individuals using the internet; it does not measure agreement with particular values, participation in a particular platform, or the quality of online relationships. [s4]\n\nConnectivity must also be distinguished from freedom to communicate. Freedom House's 2024 country report documents filtering, disruptions, and restrictions associated with Iran's National Information Network, the domestic network architecture. It also describes the use of virtual private networks to circumvent filtering. These are findings for that report's coverage period, rather than a verified account of conditions on the research cutoff date. [s5]\n\nThe Online Culture branch consequently requires both participation evidence and research into the conditions under which communication occurs. A rising usage percentage alone cannot establish unrestricted access or explain the experiences of different groups. [s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s4",
          "s5"
        ]
      },
